in and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**Who** is the subject, so use the singular present form **prepares** without an extra do.","B":"This marks the main verb as well as adding does; the form after does would have to be base.","C":"Is cannot take this base action verb in a neutral present-simple question.","D":"A to-infinitive is not a finite predicate after who."}}},{"id":"98739149-de76-5491-aad6-4a68b76de859","topicSlug":"question-forms","level":"A1","prompt":"“Did they prepare the evening meal yesterday?” Complete the positive short answer: “Yes, they ____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"were"},{"id":"B","text":"have"},{"id":"C","text":"did"},{"id":"D","text":"do"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Match the short answer’s auxiliary to the question.","explanation":"Repeat **did**, the auxiliary from the past-simple question.","keywords":["matching-short-answer"],"skill":"matching-short-answer","contextKey":"question-forms-49","fingerprint":"6cf279b4cb5db820a5ed3cc3760da62ef760f5569f34d142db36c2ece8883c91","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-question-forms"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Match the short answer’s auxiliary to the question.","Repeat **did**, the auxiliary from the past-simple question.","Choose **did**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Were does not match the auxiliary family of this question.","B":"Have would answer a perfect question, not one beginning with did.","C":"Repeat **did**, the auxiliary from the past-simple question.","D":"Do changes the short answer to present time."}}},{"id":"543e86cc-1619-514b-a632-3614a25015d3","topicSlug":"question-forms","level":"A1","prompt":"Present simple: “____ the assistant repair the rowing boat every day?”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Has"},{"id":"B","text":"Does"},{"id":"C","text":"Do"},{"id":"D","text":"Is"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Find the subject’s number, then choose **do or does**.","explanation":"The singular subject **the assistant** takes **does**; repair stays in the base form.","keywords":["present-question-auxiliary"],"skill":"present-question-auxiliary","contextKey":"question-forms-50","fingerprint":"72ebbf126cebdeaad0d551a09e309f33b7e3056666dde1cf1519b538e125855a","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-question-forms"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Find the subject’s number, then choose **do or does**.","The singular subject **the assistant** takes **does**; repair stays in the base form.","Choose **Does**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Has would require a participle for a perfect question, not this present-simple construction.","B":"The singular subject **the assistant** takes **does**; repair stays in the base form.","C":"Do does not agree with the singular third-person subject.","D":"The lexical verb is in base form, so is cannot make this present-simple question."}}},{"id":"b256b9e6-cff7-59a1-a926-17eb6414cb72","topicSlug":"question-forms","level":"A1","prompt":"Complete the past-simple question: “Why did the assistant ____ the rowing boat?”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to repair"},{"id":"B","text":"been repair"},{"id":"C","text":"repair"},{"id":"D","text":"repairs"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The past marker is already on **did**; do not mark it twice.","explanation":"**Did** carries the past tense, so choose base **repair**.","keywords":["past-question-base"],"skill":"past-question-base","contextKey":"question-forms-50","fingerprint":"107b0a72b7d9c4e395b2bbd8f19d8a1ff6e94645e22e521fef2a410561189d8d","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-question-forms"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The past marker is already on **did**; do not mark it twice.","**Did** carries the past tense, so choose base **repair**.","Choose **repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Did takes a bare infinitive, without to.","B":"Been cannot follow did to build this question.","C":"**Did** carries the past tense, so choose base **repair**.","D":"The third-person -s form cannot follow did in this question."}}},{"id":"acfc8616-0f28-5a87-af67-5c8012aeea71","topicSlug":"question-forms","level":"A1","prompt":"Ask who performs the action, in the neutral present simple: “Who ____ the rowing boat each morning?”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"is repair"},{"id":"B","text":"to repair"},{"id":"C","text":"repairs"},{"id":"D","text":"does repairs"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The missing person is the **subject**, not the object.","explanation":"**Who** is the subject, so use the singular present form **repairs** without an extra do.","keywords":["subject-question"],"skill":"subject-question","contextKey":"question-forms-50","fingerprint":"439f24a53e1a36befaff95a28cb16b2f9ce8d5f73177cd66fae75fe71cca107c","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-question-forms"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The missing person is the **subject**, not the object.","**Who** is the subject, so use the singular present form **repairs** without an extra do.","Choose **repairs**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Is cannot take this base action verb in a neutral present-simple question.","B":"A to-infinitive is not a finite predicate after who.","C":"**Who** is the subject, so use the singular present form **repairs** without an extra do.","D":"This marks the main verb as well as adding does; the form after does would have to be base."}}},{"id":"7588a433-24af-5527-a027-3f83c87b6e6b","topicSlug":"question-forms","level":"A1","prompt":"“Did they repair the rowing boat yesterday?” Complete the positive short answer: “Yes, they ____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"do"},{"id":"B","text":"were"},{"id":"C","text":"have"},{"id":"D","text":"did"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Match the short answer’s auxiliary to the question.","explanation":"Repeat **did**, the auxiliary from the past-simple question.","keywords":["matching-short-answer"],"skill":"matching-short-answer","contextKey":"question-forms-50","fingerprint":"e42dcf6c23be535adb902dccc85a322e24611eae60db52f47eaef8e86c045610","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-question-forms"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Match the short answer’s auxiliary to the question.","Repeat **did**, the auxiliary from the past-simple question.","Choose **did**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Do changes the short answer to present time.","B":"Were does not match the auxiliary family of this question.","C":"Have would answer a perfect question, not one beginning with did.","D":"Repeat **did**, the auxiliary from the past-simple question."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Build direct questions and distinguish a question about the subject from one about the object.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Use the existing auxiliary","explanation":"If a statement already has be, a modal or an auxiliary have, put the first auxiliary before the subject. The rest of the verb phrase remains after the subject. A question word, if needed, normally comes first.","pattern":"**Are** they ready? / Where **have** they gone?","commonMistakes":["Do not add do to a question already built with auxiliary be: Are they waiting?, not Do they are waiting?"],"examples":["Present simple: “**Does** the assistant prepare the visitor guide every day?”","Present simple: “**Does** the assistant restore the old portrait every day?”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Add do when needed","explanation":"For an ordinary present-simple action verb, add **do / does**. For past simple, add **did**. These auxiliaries carry tense and agreement, so the main verb uses its base form.","pattern":"Where **does** she **work**? / What **did** they **buy**?","commonMistakes":["Does she works? marks agreement twice. Keep the -s on does, not on the main verb."],"examples":["Complete the past-simple question: “Why did the assistant **prepare** the visitor guide?”","Complete the past-simple question: “Why did the assistant **restore** the old portrait?”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Subject questions","explanation":"When who or what is itself the subject, a neutral affirmative question usually has normal subject–verb order without do. Compare Who called you? with Who did you call? The person sought has a different role.","pattern":"**Who called**? / **Who did you call**?","commonMistakes":["Emphatic subject questions can use do, as in Who did call? These exercises practise the neutral pattern."],"examples":["Ask who performs the action, in the neutral present simple: “Who **prepares** the visitor guide each morning?”","Ask who performs the action, in the neutral present simple: “Who **restores** the old portrait each morning?”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Short answers","explanation":"Use a pronoun and the same auxiliary family as the question: Do they work? Yes, they do. Can she swim? No, she can’t. In a positive short answer, keep the auxiliary full rather than ending with a contraction.","pattern":"Yes, she **does**. / No, they **did not**.","commonMistakes":["Yes, she’s is not a normal complete positive short answer to Is she ready? Use Yes, she is."],"examples":["“Did they prepare the visitor guide yesterday?” Complete the positive short answer: “Yes, they **did**.”","“Did they restore the old portrait yesterday?” Complete the positive short answer: “Yes, they **did**.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["**Are** they ready? / Where **have** they gone?","Where **does** she **work**? / What **did** they **buy**?","**Who called**? / **Who did you call**?","Yes, she **does**. / No, they **did not**."]},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":["present-simple"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
